// Class 11 Accountancy practice bank, part 1: Introduction to Accounting, Theory Base, Recording of Transactions I and II, Bank Reconciliation Statement (chapters 1-5).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "accountancy", {
  "introduction-to-accounting": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The branch of accounting that is concerned with finding the cost of products and services is:", options: ["Financial accounting", "Management accounting", "Cost accounting", "Book-keeping"], answer: 2, explain: "**Cost accounting** ascertains and controls the cost of products and services. Financial accounting reports to outsiders and management accounting supplies information for managers' decisions.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Commission received by a trader for acting as an agent is classified as:", options: ["An expense", "An asset", "A liability", "Revenue"], answer: 3, explain: "Commission received is earned from regular business activity, so it is **revenue** (income). It increases capital.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a current liability?", options: ["Bills payable", "Bank loan repayable after five years", "Capital", "Debentures due after ten years"], answer: 0, explain: "**Bills payable** are due within a year, so they are a current liability. Long-term loans and debentures are non-current liabilities, and capital is the owner's claim.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Capital of the proprietor can be found as:", options: ["Assets + Liabilities", "Liabilities − Assets", "Assets − Liabilities", "Revenue − Expenses"], answer: 2, explain: "From Assets = Liabilities + Capital, **Capital = Assets − Liabilities**. Revenue − Expenses gives profit, not capital.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a limitation of accounting?", options: ["It records only transactions that can be measured in money", "It provides a systematic record of transactions", "It shows profit or loss of the business", "It helps in settling tax matters"], answer: 0, explain: "Accounting records only **monetary** transactions, uses historical cost and ignores price-level changes. The other options are objectives or uses of accounting.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Amit started business with ₹2,00,000. During the year he introduced ₹50,000 more, withdrew ₹30,000 for personal use and earned a profit of ₹80,000. His closing capital is:", options: ["₹3,60,000", "₹3,00,000", "₹2,20,000", "₹3,30,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Closing capital = Opening capital + Additional capital + Profit − Drawings = 2,00,000 + 50,000 + 80,000 − 30,000 = **₹3,00,000**. (₹3,30,000 forgets to deduct drawings.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "The assets of a firm are ₹5,40,000 and its outside liabilities are ₹1,90,000. The proprietor's capital is:", options: ["₹7,30,000", "₹1,90,000", "₹5,40,000", "₹3,50,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Capital = Assets − Liabilities = 5,40,000 − 1,90,000 = **₹3,50,000**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Trade discount is not recorded in the books of account.\nReason (R): Trade discount is deducted from the list price on the invoice itself, so only the net amount is actually payable.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Because the invoice already shows the net amount, there is nothing extra to record. Cash discount, in contrast, depends on prompt payment and is recorded.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A bank is considering a loan to Sandhu Stores. The loan officer wants to know whether the store will be able to pay interest regularly and repay the loan on time.\nThe bank is which type of user, and what is its main interest in the accounts?", options: ["Internal user; interested in profitability", "External user; interested in ability to repay", "External user; interested in growth of share price", "Internal user; interested in ability to repay"], answer: 1, explain: "A lender is outside the business, so it is an **external user**, and its main concern is the firm's **ability to repay** interest and principal.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Ritu buys a delivery van for ₹6,00,000, pays ₹15,000 for repairs in the same year and ₹8,000 for fuel.\nThe amount that is capital expenditure is:", options: ["₹23,000", "₹6,15,000", "₹6,23,000", "₹6,00,000"], answer: 3, explain: "The van gives benefit for many years, so its cost of **₹6,00,000** is capital expenditure. Repairs and fuel are used up within the year and are revenue expenditure.", level: 2 },
      { q: "On 1 April the assets and liabilities of a firm were ₹3,00,000 and ₹1,00,000. During the year the owner introduced ₹40,000 and withdrew ₹25,000. On 31 March assets were ₹5,10,000 and liabilities ₹1,60,000. The profit for the year is:", options: ["₹1,35,000", "₹1,50,000", "₹1,10,000", "₹1,60,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Opening capital = 2,00,000; closing capital = 3,50,000. Closing = Opening + Additional + Profit − Drawings, so 3,50,000 = 2,00,000 + 40,000 + P − 25,000 and P = **₹1,35,000**. (₹1,50,000 is just the change in capital.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A trader buys goods with a list price of ₹40,000 at a trade discount of 10%. The supplier allows a further cash discount of 2% on the net price for payment within 10 days. The amount paid within the period is:", options: ["₹36,000", "₹39,200", "₹35,280", "₹35,200"], answer: 2, explain: "Net price = 40,000 − 10% = ₹36,000. Cash discount = 2% of 36,000 = ₹720. Amount paid = 36,000 − 720 = **₹35,280**. (₹39,200 takes 2% off the list price.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A trader starts with cash ₹80,000, buys goods ₹30,000 on credit, sells goods costing ₹12,000 for ₹18,000 on credit and pays rent ₹2,000 in cash. His capital at the end is:", options: ["₹86,000", "₹84,000", "₹78,000", "₹1,14,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Profit on sale = 18,000 − 12,000 = ₹6,000; rent reduces capital by ₹2,000. Capital = 80,000 + 6,000 − 2,000 = **₹84,000**. Check: assets = cash 78,000 + stock 18,000 + debtors 18,000 = 1,14,000 = creditors 30,000 + capital 84,000.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Drawings increase the capital of the proprietor.\nReason (R): Drawings are cash or goods taken by the owner from the business for personal use.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is the definition of drawings. Since the owner takes value out of the business, drawings **reduce** capital, so A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is meant by drawings?", a: "Drawings are the cash or goods withdrawn by the proprietor from the business for his personal use. They reduce the capital.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the accounting equation.", a: "Assets = Liabilities + Capital (or Capital = Assets − Liabilities).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State two differences between capital expenditure and revenue expenditure.", a: "(1) Capital expenditure gives benefit for **more than one year** (for example, buying a machine); revenue expenditure is consumed **within the year** (for example, wages, repairs).\n(2) Capital expenditure appears as an **asset** in the balance sheet; revenue expenditure is charged against revenue in the profit and loss account.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Classify the following as current or non-current assets: land, debtors, stock of goods, machinery.", a: "Non-current assets: **land** and **machinery** (held for long-term use). Current assets: **debtors** and **stock of goods** (they turn into cash within a year).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain any three limitations of accounting.", a: "(1) It records only **monetary** transactions; matters like the skill of workers are left out.\n(2) It is based on **historical cost**, so assets are not shown at their current value.\n(3) It ignores **price-level changes** (inflation), and some figures, such as depreciation, depend on personal judgement.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "On 1 April 2024 Rakesh's assets were ₹5,20,000 and liabilities ₹1,20,000. During the year he introduced ₹60,000 more capital and withdrew ₹90,000. On 31 March 2025 his assets were ₹7,40,000 and liabilities ₹1,50,000. Find his profit for the year.", a: "Opening capital = 5,20,000 − 1,20,000 = ₹4,00,000.\nClosing capital = 7,40,000 − 1,50,000 = ₹5,90,000.\nClosing capital = Opening + Additional + Profit − Drawings\n5,90,000 = 4,00,000 + 60,000 + Profit − 90,000\nProfit = 5,90,000 − 4,00,000 − 60,000 + 90,000 = **₹1,20,000**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name any three users of accounting information and state what each one wants to know.", a: "(1) **Management** (internal): profit, cost and financial position for planning and control.\n(2) **Investors**: profitability and growth to decide whether to invest.\n(3) **Banks and creditors**: the firm's ability to pay interest and repay dues.\n(Tax authorities, who want correct income for tax, may be given instead.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the four qualitative characteristics of accounting information.", a: "(1) **Reliability**: the information is free from error and bias, can be verified and faithfully shows the facts.\n(2) **Relevance**: it is available in time and helps users predict outcomes or confirm earlier expectations.\n(3) **Understandability**: it is presented so that users understand it as the preparer intended.\n(4) **Comparability**: users can compare the firm's performance over years and with other firms.\n(5) Together these make the information useful for decisions; an item that fails any of them reduces the value of the statements.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Show the effect of the following transactions on the accounting equation: (i) commenced business with cash ₹1,50,000; (ii) purchased goods on credit ₹40,000; (iii) purchased furniture for cash ₹25,000; (iv) sold goods costing ₹20,000 for ₹30,000 on credit; (v) paid rent ₹5,000; (vi) withdrew cash ₹10,000 for personal use.", a: "(i) Cash 1,50,000 = Capital 1,50,000\n(ii) Cash 1,50,000 + Stock 40,000 = Creditors 40,000 + Capital 1,50,000 (1,90,000)\n(iii) Cash 1,25,000 + Stock 40,000 + Furniture 25,000 = Creditors 40,000 + Capital 1,50,000 (1,90,000)\n(iv) Cash 1,25,000 + Stock 20,000 + Debtors 30,000 + Furniture 25,000 = Creditors 40,000 + Capital 1,60,000 (2,00,000), profit ₹10,000 added\n(v) Cash 1,20,000 + Stock 20,000 + Debtors 30,000 + Furniture 25,000 = Creditors 40,000 + Capital 1,55,000 (1,95,000)\n(vi) Cash 1,10,000 + Stock 20,000 + Debtors 30,000 + Furniture 25,000 = Creditors 40,000 + Capital 1,45,000 (1,85,000)\nBoth sides agree at ₹1,85,000.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Harpreet runs a stationery shop. On 1 April 2024 her assets were ₹6,50,000 and her liabilities ₹2,10,000. During the year she introduced ₹40,000 more, withdrew ₹60,000 for personal use and earned a profit of ₹1,80,000. She also took a bank loan repayable after five years.\n(i) Find her capital on 1 April 2024.\n(ii) Find her capital on 31 March 2025.\n(iii) Is the bank loan a current or a non-current liability? Give a reason.", a: "(i) Opening capital = 6,50,000 − 2,10,000 = **₹4,40,000**.\n(ii) Closing capital = 4,40,000 + 40,000 + 1,80,000 − 60,000 = **₹6,00,000**.\n(iii) **Non-current liability**, because it is repayable after more than one year.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "theory-base-of-accounting": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Fixed assets are shown at cost less depreciation and not at their expected sale value because of the:", options: ["Conservatism concept", "Going concern concept", "Materiality concept", "Matching concept"], answer: 1, explain: "Under the **going concern** concept the business is assumed to continue, so assets held for use are shown at cost less depreciation rather than at what they would fetch if sold.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Goods and Services Tax came into force in India on:", options: ["1 April 2016", "1 April 2017", "1 July 2017", "1 January 2018"], answer: 2, explain: "GST was introduced in India on **1 July 2017**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Under the GST system, the tax on an intra-state sale that goes to the State Government is:", options: ["CGST", "IGST", "SGST", "Customs duty"], answer: 2, explain: "On an intra-state supply, **CGST** goes to the Centre and **SGST** goes to the State. IGST applies to inter-state supplies.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Recording a transaction only when cash is actually received or paid is called the:", options: ["Cash basis of accounting", "Accrual basis of accounting", "Cost basis of accounting", "Dual basis of accounting"], answer: 0, explain: "On the **cash basis** only actual receipts and payments are recorded, so there are no outstanding or prepaid items.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Accounting Standards in India are issued by the Accounting Standards Board, which was set up in:", options: ["1949", "1966", "2005", "1977"], answer: 3, explain: "The ASB of the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI) was set up in **1977**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Case: Mehra Software employs a highly skilled team of programmers whose talent is the main strength of the firm. Yet the balance sheet does not show any value for these employees.\nThis is because of the:", options: ["Money measurement concept", "Business entity concept", "Accounting period concept", "Dual aspect concept"], answer: 0, explain: "The **money measurement** concept records only those events that can be expressed in money. The skill of employees cannot be measured in money, so it is not recorded.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Closing stock is valued at cost or net realisable value, whichever is lower.\nReason (R): The conservatism concept requires that anticipated profits be recorded but anticipated losses be ignored.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true. R is false: conservatism says do **not** anticipate profits but **provide** for all possible losses.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "The cost of a trader's closing stock is ₹80,000 and its net realisable value is ₹72,000. The stock is valued at:", options: ["₹80,000", "₹76,000", "₹72,000", "₹8,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Under conservatism, stock is valued at the lower of cost and net realisable value, that is **₹72,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A dealer in Haryana sells goods of ₹25,000 to a customer in Haryana. GST is charged at 18%. The SGST collected is:", options: ["₹4,500", "₹2,250", "₹1,125", "₹2,500"], answer: 1, explain: "Total GST = 18% of 25,000 = ₹4,500. For an intra-state sale it is split equally: CGST ₹2,250 and SGST **₹2,250**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A shopkeeper sold goods on credit to a customer on 28 March 2025. The customer paid the money on 10 April 2025.\nIn which concept is it said that the sale is the income of the year ending 31 March 2025?", options: ["Cost concept", "Cash basis", "Materiality", "Revenue recognition"], answer: 3, explain: "Under the **revenue recognition** concept, revenue is recognised when it is earned (goods sold), not when cash is received.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A credit sale made in March is treated as income of that year even if cash is received in May.\nReason (R): The cash basis of accounting records income only when cash is received.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "A is true under the accrual basis and revenue recognition. R is a true statement about the cash basis, but it is the **opposite** principle and does not explain A.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A Delhi dealer buys goods of ₹1,00,000 in Delhi and sells them within Delhi for ₹1,50,000. GST is 18% on both (CGST 9% and SGST 9%). The total GST payable to the government (CGST and SGST together) after input tax credit is:", options: ["₹27,000", "₹4,500", "₹9,000", "₹18,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Output CGST = 9% of 1,50,000 = ₹13,500; input CGST = 9% of 1,00,000 = ₹9,000, so CGST payable ₹4,500. SGST is the same, ₹4,500. Total = **₹9,000**. (₹27,000 is output tax only; ₹18,000 is input tax only.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A Punjab dealer buys goods of ₹40,000 within Punjab (GST 12%: CGST 6% and SGST 6%) and sells them for ₹60,000 to a dealer in Haryana (IGST 12%). The net IGST payable after using the input credit is:", options: ["₹7,200", "₹2,400", "₹4,800", "₹9,600"], answer: 1, explain: "Output IGST = 12% of 60,000 = ₹7,200. Input CGST ₹2,400 and input SGST ₹2,400 can both be set off against IGST (total ₹4,800). Payable = 7,200 − 4,800 = **₹2,400**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A firm's rent of ₹12,000 for the year ended 31 March was paid on 5 April. All other items are the same. Compared with the cash basis, the profit under the accrual basis for that year is:", options: ["Higher by ₹12,000", "The same", "Lower by ₹1,000", "Lower by ₹12,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Under accrual the unpaid rent is an expense of the year, so profit is **lower by ₹12,000**. Under the cash basis the rent is ignored until it is paid.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the concept under which only transactions that can be expressed in money are recorded.", a: "The **money measurement** concept.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State one benefit of accounting standards.", a: "They bring uniformity in accounting practices, so financial statements of different firms become comparable (any one: better comparability, greater credibility, fewer chances of manipulation).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between CGST and IGST (two points).", a: "(1) **CGST** is levied on an **intra-state** supply (within a state); **IGST** is levied on an **inter-state** supply.\n(2) CGST is collected by the Centre together with SGST at half the rate each; **IGST** is collected by the Centre at the full rate, and its share is later passed to the destination state.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain the money measurement concept. Give two examples of things it leaves out of the books.", a: "Only transactions and events that can be **expressed in money** are recorded in the books of account, which gives a common unit for adding different items.\nExamples that are not recorded: the skill or honesty of the employees, a strike in the factory, the reputation of the manager (any two).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain the conservatism concept with two examples.", a: "**Conservatism (prudence)** means do not anticipate profits but provide for all possible losses.\nExample 1: closing stock is valued at **cost or net realisable value, whichever is lower**.\nExample 2: a **provision for doubtful debts** is created for expected losses from debtors.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Shiv Traders, Jaipur, purchased goods worth ₹80,000 from a Jaipur supplier and sold goods worth ₹1,20,000 within Jaipur. GST on both is 18% (CGST 9%, SGST 9%). Calculate the CGST and SGST payable after input tax credit.", a: "Output CGST = 9% of 1,20,000 = ₹10,800; Output SGST = ₹10,800.\nInput CGST = 9% of 80,000 = ₹7,200; Input SGST = ₹7,200.\nCGST payable = 10,800 − 7,200 = **₹3,600**.\nSGST payable = 10,800 − 7,200 = **₹3,600**.\n(Total GST payable = ₹7,200.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain any three benefits of accounting standards.", a: "(1) **Uniformity**: all firms follow the same policies for recognising, measuring and presenting items.\n(2) **Comparability**: statements of different firms and different years can be compared.\n(3) **Credibility**: users trust statements prepared as per standards, and there is less scope for manipulation.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain any five basic accounting concepts.", a: "(1) **Business entity**: the business is separate from the owner, so capital is a liability of the business.\n(2) **Going concern**: the business will continue for a long time, so fixed assets are shown at cost less depreciation.\n(3) **Cost concept**: assets are recorded at the cost paid, not at market value.\n(4) **Dual aspect**: every transaction has two equal effects, so Assets = Liabilities + Capital always.\n(5) **Accounting period**: the life of the business is divided into periods (usually a year) for finding profit and position.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A trader gives the following for the year ended 31 March 2025: cash received from customers ₹4,50,000; credit sales not yet received ₹60,000; expenses paid in cash ₹2,80,000 (this includes ₹20,000 paid in advance for the next year); expenses outstanding ₹30,000. Find the profit on (a) the cash basis and (b) the accrual basis, and state which gives the truer profit.", a: "(a) Cash basis: only cash receipts and payments.\nProfit = 4,50,000 − 2,80,000 = **₹1,70,000**.\n(b) Accrual basis:\nRevenue = 4,50,000 + 60,000 = ₹5,10,000\nExpenses = 2,80,000 − 20,000 (prepaid) + 30,000 (outstanding) = ₹2,90,000\nProfit = 5,10,000 − 2,90,000 = **₹2,20,000**.\nCheck: 1,70,000 + 60,000 + 20,000 − 30,000 = 2,20,000.\nThe **accrual basis** gives the truer profit because it matches the revenue earned and expenses incurred in the same year.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Kumar Electronics, Ludhiana (Punjab), sold a television for ₹40,000 (before tax) to a customer in Ludhiana and an air conditioner for ₹30,000 (before tax) to a dealer in Shimla (Himachal Pradesh). GST on both items is 18%.\n(i) Calculate the CGST and SGST on the television.\n(ii) Calculate the IGST on the air conditioner.\n(iii) Why can CGST not be set off against SGST?", a: "(i) GST = 18% of 40,000 = ₹7,200. This is an intra-state sale, so **CGST = ₹3,600 and SGST = ₹3,600**.\n(ii) The sale to Shimla is inter-state, so **IGST = 18% of 30,000 = ₹5,400**.\n(iii) CGST belongs to the Centre and SGST to the State Government, which are different governments, so one cannot be set off against the other.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "recording-of-transactions-1": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A debit note is sent by:", options: ["The seller to the buyer when goods are returned", "The buyer to the seller when goods are returned", "The bank to its customer", "The customer to the bank"], answer: 1, explain: "When a buyer returns goods to the supplier, he sends a **debit note** showing that the supplier's account has been debited. A credit note is sent by the seller.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When the proprietor takes cash from the business for personal use, the account debited is:", options: ["Drawings A/c", "Capital A/c", "Purchases A/c", "Sales A/c"], answer: 0, explain: "Withdrawals for personal use are debited to **Drawings A/c** and cash is credited.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The book of original entry in which transactions are first recorded in date order is the:", options: ["Ledger", "Trial balance", "Journal", "Balance sheet"], answer: 2, explain: "The **journal** records transactions chronologically for the first time; they are then posted to the ledger.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Goods bought on credit from Ravi. The account to be credited is:", options: ["Purchases A/c", "Cash A/c", "Capital A/c", "Ravi's A/c"], answer: 3, explain: "Purchases A/c is debited; Ravi is a supplier who gave the goods, so **Ravi's A/c** is credited.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following accounts normally shows a credit balance?", options: ["Furniture", "Debtors", "Creditors", "Rent paid"], answer: 2, explain: "**Creditors** are a liability and show a credit balance. Furniture, debtors and rent paid (an expense) have debit balances.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Goods of ₹20,000 are sold on credit to Sunil within the state. CGST and SGST are charged at 9% each. The amount debited to Sunil's account is:", options: ["₹20,000", "₹21,800", "₹18,200", "₹23,600"], answer: 3, explain: "CGST = SGST = 9% of 20,000 = ₹1,800 each. Sunil owes 20,000 + 1,800 + 1,800 = **₹23,600**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Meena Boutique pays salary of ₹15,000 by cheque.\nThe effect on the accounting equation is:", options: ["Assets fall by ₹15,000 and liabilities fall by ₹15,000", "Assets fall by ₹15,000 and capital falls by ₹15,000", "Assets rise by ₹15,000 and capital rises by ₹15,000", "One asset rises and another falls by ₹15,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Bank (an asset) falls and salary is an expense, which reduces **capital**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Output CGST A/c is shown as an asset.\nReason (R): GST collected on sales has to be paid over to the government.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. Because the amount is payable to the government, Output CGST is a **liability**, so A is false. Input CGST is the asset.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "At the end of a month the Output CGST balance is ₹7,500 and the Input CGST balance is ₹4,200. After setting off, the amount of CGST to be paid in cash or by bank is:", options: ["₹3,300", "₹4,200", "₹7,500", "₹11,700"], answer: 0, explain: "Input CGST is set off against output CGST: 7,500 − 4,200 = **₹3,300** is paid.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Himanshu Stores bought 100 shirts from Mohan on credit and found 10 of them defective. The 10 shirts, valued at ₹5,000, were returned to Mohan along with a debit note.\nWhich account is debited for this return?", options: ["Purchases Return A/c", "Sales Return A/c", "Mohan's A/c", "Cash A/c"], answer: 2, explain: "Himanshu Stores owes Mohan less now, so **Mohan's A/c is debited** and Purchases Return A/c is credited.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Input SGST cannot be set off against output CGST.\nReason (R): CGST and SGST are levied by and belong to different governments.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "CGST goes to the Centre and SGST to the State, so credit of one cannot be used for the other. Input SGST can be set off only against output SGST (and IGST).", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "On 1 April Ram's account showed a debit balance of ₹12,000. During the month goods of ₹30,000 were sold to him on credit; he paid ₹25,000 in cash, was allowed a discount of ₹500 and returned goods of ₹2,000. The closing balance of Ram's account is:", options: ["₹15,000", "₹16,500", "₹14,500", "₹17,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Debit side: 12,000 + 30,000 = 42,000. Credit side: 25,000 + 500 + 2,000 = 27,500. Balance = **₹14,500** (debit). (₹15,000 forgets the discount.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Goods of list price ₹60,000 are bought on credit from Gupta at 20% trade discount. CGST and SGST are 6% each, charged on the price after trade discount. The amount credited to Gupta is:", options: ["₹60,000", "₹53,760", "₹48,000", "₹67,200"], answer: 1, explain: "Net price = 60,000 − 12,000 = ₹48,000. CGST = SGST = 6% of 48,000 = ₹2,880 each. Gupta is credited 48,000 + 5,760 = **₹53,760**. (₹67,200 wrongly charges GST on the list price.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A firm has assets ₹5,00,000, liabilities ₹1,50,000 and capital ₹3,50,000. It pays ₹40,000 to a creditor in cash and then sells goods costing ₹30,000 for ₹45,000 in cash. The total assets after both transactions are:", options: ["₹4,60,000", "₹5,15,000", "₹4,75,000", "₹5,05,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Payment to creditor: assets 5,00,000 − 40,000 = 4,60,000. Cash sale: cash +45,000, stock −30,000, so assets rise by 15,000 to **₹4,75,000**. Check: liabilities 1,10,000 + capital 3,65,000 = 4,75,000.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the document that a seller sends to a customer when the customer returns goods.", a: "A **credit note**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give the journal entry when the proprietor starts business by bringing ₹2,00,000 in cash.", a: "Cash A/c Dr. 2,00,000\n    To Capital A/c 2,00,000", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Journalise: (a) goods costing ₹3,000 taken by the proprietor for personal use; (b) rent of ₹4,000 paid by cheque.", a: "(a) Drawings A/c Dr. 3,000\n        To Purchases A/c 3,000\n(b) Rent A/c Dr. 4,000\n        To Bank A/c 4,000", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is Input CGST shown as an asset while Output CGST is shown as a liability?", a: "**Input CGST** is the GST the firm has already paid on purchases, and it can be claimed as credit against the tax payable, so it is an **asset**.\n**Output CGST** is the GST collected from customers on sales, which the firm must pay to the government, so it is a **liability**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Pass journal entries for: (i) purchased goods from Anil on credit for ₹40,000, GST 18% (CGST 9% + SGST 9%); (ii) sold goods to Bhavna of another state for ₹60,000 on credit, IGST 12%; (iii) paid Anil ₹46,000 in full settlement.", a: "(i) Purchases A/c Dr. 40,000\n    Input CGST A/c Dr. 3,600\n    Input SGST A/c Dr. 3,600\n        To Anil 47,200\n(ii) Bhavna Dr. 67,200\n        To Sales A/c 60,000\n        To Output IGST A/c 7,200\n(iii) Anil Dr. 47,200\n        To Cash A/c 46,000\n        To Discount Received A/c 1,200\nEach entry balances (47,200; 67,200; 47,200).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "At the end of the month, the balances are: Output CGST ₹12,000, Output SGST ₹12,000, Input CGST ₹9,000, Input SGST ₹14,000. Pass the entries to set off the input credit and to pay the tax due by bank. What happens to the balance of the remaining credit?", a: "Output CGST A/c Dr. 9,000\n    To Input CGST A/c 9,000\nOutput SGST A/c Dr. 12,000\n    To Input SGST A/c 12,000\nOutput CGST A/c Dr. 3,000\n    To Bank A/c 3,000 (CGST still payable: 12,000 − 9,000)\nInput SGST of ₹2,000 (14,000 − 12,000) is left over. It **cannot** be set off against CGST, so it is carried forward to be used against future SGST (or IGST) liability.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "On 1 April Ram's account had a debit balance of ₹12,000. On 10 April goods of ₹30,000 were sold to him on credit. On 20 April he paid ₹25,000 in cash and was allowed a discount of ₹500. On 25 April he returned goods of ₹2,000. Prepare Ram's account and balance it.", a: "Ram's Account\nDr.                                      Cr.\nApr 1  To Balance b/d      12,000   Apr 20 By Cash                25,000\nApr 10 To Sales            30,000   Apr 20 By Discount Allowed      500\n                                    Apr 25 By Sales Return        2,000\n                                    Apr 30 By Balance c/d        14,500\n                           42,000                                  42,000\nMay 1  To Balance b/d      14,500\nRam owes ₹14,500.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Pass journal entries (with narrations omitted) for the following transactions of Kunal in April 2025: (1) 1 Apr started business with cash ₹1,50,000 and furniture ₹50,000; (2) 3 Apr purchased goods from Ravi on credit ₹40,000; (3) 5 Apr sold goods for cash ₹25,000; (4) 10 Apr paid Ravi ₹39,000 in full settlement; (5) 15 Apr took goods of ₹2,000 for personal use; (6) 20 Apr paid salary ₹8,000 in cash.", a: "(1) Cash A/c Dr. 1,50,000\n    Furniture A/c Dr. 50,000\n        To Capital A/c 2,00,000\n(2) Purchases A/c Dr. 40,000\n        To Ravi 40,000\n(3) Cash A/c Dr. 25,000\n        To Sales A/c 25,000\n(4) Ravi Dr. 40,000\n        To Cash A/c 39,000\n        To Discount Received A/c 1,000\n(5) Drawings A/c Dr. 2,000\n        To Purchases A/c 2,000\n(6) Salary A/c Dr. 8,000\n        To Cash A/c 8,000", marks: 5 },
      { q: "From the following transactions of Tara, prepare the Cash Account in the ledger and balance it: 1 Apr started business with cash ₹2,00,000; 4 Apr purchased goods for cash ₹60,000; 8 Apr cash sales ₹45,000; 12 Apr paid rent ₹6,000; 18 Apr received from Dev ₹18,000; 25 Apr paid wages ₹4,000; 28 Apr withdrew ₹10,000 for personal use.", a: "Cash Account\nDr.                                         Cr.\nApr 1  To Capital        2,00,000   Apr 4  By Purchases        60,000\nApr 8  To Sales             45,000   Apr 12 By Rent               6,000\nApr 18 To Dev               18,000   Apr 25 By Wages              4,000\n                                     Apr 28 By Drawings          10,000\n                                     Apr 30 By Balance c/d    1,83,000\n                         2,63,000                              2,63,000\nMay 1  To Balance b/d    1,83,000\nDebit side = 2,00,000 + 45,000 + 18,000 = 2,63,000. Credit side = 60,000 + 6,000 + 4,000 + 10,000 = 80,000. Balance = 2,63,000 − 80,000 = **₹1,83,000** (debit).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Gurmeet Traders, Amritsar, bought goods worth ₹1,00,000 on credit from a supplier in Amritsar, GST 12% (CGST 6%, SGST 6%). The same month it sold goods worth ₹1,50,000 in Amritsar for cash, GST 12%.\n(i) Pass the journal entry for the purchase.\n(ii) How much cash did it receive on the sale?\n(iii) Find the net CGST payable for the month.", a: "(i) Purchases A/c Dr. 1,00,000\n    Input CGST A/c Dr. 6,000\n    Input SGST A/c Dr. 6,000\n        To Supplier 1,12,000\n(ii) GST on sale = 12% of 1,50,000 = ₹18,000. Cash received = 1,50,000 + 18,000 = **₹1,68,000**.\n(iii) Output CGST = 6% of 1,50,000 = ₹9,000. Net CGST payable = 9,000 − 6,000 (input) = **₹3,000**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "recording-of-transactions-2": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A credit sale of goods is first recorded in the:", options: ["Purchases book", "Cash book", "Sales book", "Journal proper"], answer: 2, explain: "Credit sales of goods are entered in the **sales book** from the invoices issued to customers.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Goods returned by the firm to its supplier are recorded in the:", options: ["Sales return book", "Purchases return book", "Purchases book", "Journal proper"], answer: 1, explain: "Goods returned to suppliers are return outwards, recorded in the **purchases return book** on the basis of debit notes.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The petty cash book is maintained by the:", options: ["Proprietor", "Auditor", "Petty cashier", "Bank manager"], answer: 2, explain: "Small routine payments are recorded by the **petty cashier**, who is given an imprest by the head cashier.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The letter 'C' written in the L.F. column of a cash book indicates a:", options: ["Contra entry", "Cash purchase", "Credit note", "Compound entry"], answer: 0, explain: "'C' marks a **contra entry**, one that affects both the cash and bank columns, such as depositing cash into the bank.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The cash book is:", options: ["Only a ledger account", "Only a book of original entry", "A statement prepared by the bank", "A book of original entry as well as a ledger account"], answer: 3, explain: "Cash transactions are first recorded in it, and it is also balanced like the Cash A/c, so it is **both**; no separate Cash A/c is needed.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The total of the purchases book is posted to the:", options: ["Credit of Purchases A/c", "Debit of Purchases A/c", "Debit of each supplier's account", "Credit of Sales A/c"], answer: 1, explain: "The periodic total is **debited to Purchases A/c**, while each supplier's account is credited individually.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A firm has cash ₹12,000 and a bank balance of ₹40,000 (Dr.). It deposits a cheque of ₹5,000 received from a customer and withdraws ₹8,000 from the bank for office use. The closing bank balance is:", options: ["₹45,000", "₹32,000", "₹37,000", "₹53,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Bank = 40,000 + 5,000 (cheque deposited) − 8,000 (withdrawal) = **₹37,000**. Cash rises to 12,000 + 8,000 = 20,000.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The cash column of a cash book can never show a credit balance.\nReason (R): The cash paid out cannot exceed the cash available.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Payments can only be made out of cash that is actually available, so the cash column is either debit or nil. Hence R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: The petty cashier of Dhillon Enterprises received an imprest of ₹6,000 on 1 June and spent ₹3,450 on various small items during the month.\nHow much will he be reimbursed at the end of the month?", options: ["₹6,000", "₹2,550", "₹3,450", "₹9,450"], answer: 2, explain: "Under the imprest system he is reimbursed the amount he spent, **₹3,450**, so that he again holds ₹6,000.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Sachdeva Garments sold 20 jackets to Bharat on credit. The list price is ₹2,000 per jacket and a trade discount of 15% is allowed. GST is ignored.\nThe amount entered in the sales book is:", options: ["₹40,000", "₹6,000", "₹36,000", "₹34,000"], answer: 3, explain: "List price = 20 × 2,000 = ₹40,000. Trade discount = 15% = ₹6,000. The sales book shows the net amount **₹34,000**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Cash purchases of goods are recorded in the purchases book.\nReason (R): The purchases book records only credit purchases of goods.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. Cash purchases go to the **cash book**, so A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "The opening balances of a firm are cash ₹8,000 and bank ₹22,000 (Dr.). Then: cash sales ₹14,000; a cheque of ₹9,000 received from Raman is deposited; rent of ₹5,000 paid by cheque; wages of ₹3,000 paid in cash; cash of ₹10,000 deposited into the bank; and Raman's cheque is dishonoured. The closing cash and bank balances are:", options: ["₹9,000 and ₹32,000", "₹9,000 and ₹23,000", "₹19,000 and ₹23,000", "₹12,000 and ₹32,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Cash = 8,000 + 14,000 − 3,000 − 10,000 = **₹9,000**. Bank = 22,000 + 9,000 − 5,000 + 10,000 − 9,000 (dishonour) = **₹23,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A petty cashier holds an imprest of ₹10,000 and has ₹1,800 left at the end of the month. The amount of reimbursement and the total of the expense columns of the petty cash book are:", options: ["₹1,800 and ₹1,800", "₹10,000 and ₹8,200", "₹8,200 and ₹8,200", "₹8,200 and ₹10,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Amount spent = 10,000 − 1,800 = ₹8,200. That is the total of the expense columns and also the **reimbursement**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A firm received three invoices: (1) goods of list price ₹20,000 less 10% trade discount; (2) goods of ₹15,000 net; (3) office furniture ₹12,000. The total posted from the purchases book to Purchases A/c is:", options: ["₹33,000", "₹35,000", "₹45,000", "₹47,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Invoice 1 is entered at ₹18,000 (net of trade discount) and invoice 2 at ₹15,000: 18,000 + 15,000 = **₹33,000**. Furniture is an asset and goes to the journal proper.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Which document is the basis for entries in the sales return book?", a: "A **credit note** issued by the firm to the customer who returned the goods.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is meant by an imprest in a petty cash book?", a: "The fixed amount given to the petty cashier at the start of a period, which is topped up at the end by the amount he spent.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the purchases book and the purchases return book (two points).", a: "(1) The **purchases book** records credit purchases of goods; the **purchases return book** records goods returned to suppliers.\n(2) The purchases book is prepared from invoices received; the purchases return book from debit notes issued. Its total is credited to Purchases Return A/c.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the book in which each is recorded: (a) sale of old furniture on credit; (b) purchase of goods for cash; (c) goods destroyed by fire; (d) credit purchase of goods.", a: "(a) **Journal proper** (it is an asset, not goods).\n(b) **Cash book**.\n(c) **Journal proper**.\n(d) **Purchases book**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Enter the following in the purchases book of Arora & Co. and show the posting of the total: 3 Jan purchased from Kiran Traders 50 shirts at ₹400 each less 10% trade discount; 12 Jan from Lalit & Sons 20 jackets at ₹900 each less 5% trade discount; 20 Jan from Kiran Traders 30 shirts at ₹380 each (no discount).", a: "Purchases Book\nDate    Supplier           List price   Trade disc.   Net amount\n3 Jan   Kiran Traders         20,000       2,000        18,000\n12 Jan  Lalit & Sons          18,000         900        17,100\n20 Jan  Kiran Traders         11,400           —        11,400\n                                          Total        46,500\nPosting: Purchases A/c Dr. 46,500; credit Kiran Traders 29,400 (18,000 + 11,400) and Lalit & Sons 17,100. Credits 29,400 + 17,100 = 46,500, equal to the debit.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prepare a simple cash book from the following: opening cash ₹10,000; cash sales ₹30,000; paid rent ₹5,000; purchased goods for cash ₹12,000; received from Vikas ₹8,000; paid salaries ₹9,000.", a: "Cash Book\nDr.                                 Cr.\nTo Balance b/d    10,000    By Rent                 5,000\nTo Sales          30,000    By Purchases           12,000\nTo Vikas           8,000    By Salaries             9,000\n                            By Balance c/d         22,000\n                  48,000                           48,000\nReceipts = 10,000 + 30,000 + 8,000 = 48,000. Payments = 5,000 + 12,000 + 9,000 = 26,000. Balance = 48,000 − 26,000 = **₹22,000**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A petty cashier received an imprest of ₹4,000 on 1 January. His payments were: 2 Jan postage ₹250; 5 Jan stationery ₹600; 9 Jan conveyance ₹340; 14 Jan cartage ₹410; 21 Jan stationery ₹700; 28 Jan postage ₹150. Prepare the analytical petty cash book column totals, and state the reimbursement and the closing balance.", a: "Analytical column totals:\nPostage = 250 + 150 = 400\nStationery = 600 + 700 = 1,300\nConveyance = 340\nCartage = 410\nTotal payments = 400 + 1,300 + 340 + 410 = 2,450 (check: 250 + 600 + 340 + 410 + 700 + 150 = 2,450)\nCash in hand at the end = 4,000 − 2,450 = ₹1,550.\nReimbursement on 31 Jan = **₹2,450**; the cashier starts February with ₹1,550 + 2,450 = ₹4,000.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prepare a cash book with cash and bank columns: 1 Jan cash ₹15,000 and bank ₹60,000; 3 Jan cash sales ₹20,000; 5 Jan cash deposited into bank ₹25,000; 8 Jan cheque received from Seema ₹18,000 and deposited the same day; 12 Jan rent paid by cheque ₹6,000; 15 Jan goods purchased by cheque ₹30,000; 20 Jan cash withdrawn from bank for office use ₹10,000; 25 Jan wages paid in cash ₹7,000; 28 Jan Seema's cheque was dishonoured.", a: "Cash Book\nDr.                                         Cr.\n              Cash    Bank                           Cash    Bank\nBalance b/d  15,000  60,000   Bank (C)            25,000       —\nSales        20,000       —   Rent                     —   6,000\nCash (C)          —  25,000   Purchases                —  30,000\nSeema             —  18,000   Cash (C)                 —  10,000\nBank (C)     10,000       —   Wages                7,000       —\n                              Seema (dishonour)        —  18,000\n                              Balance c/d          13,000  39,000\n             45,000 1,03,000                       45,000 1,03,000\nCash: 45,000 − (25,000 + 7,000) = **₹13,000**.\nBank: 1,03,000 − (6,000 + 30,000 + 10,000 + 18,000) = **₹39,000** (debit, favourable).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Pass journal proper entries for the following transactions of Bedi Traders: (i) purchased office furniture on credit from Modern Furnishers for ₹36,000; (ii) sold an old computer (book value ₹8,000) on credit to Anil for ₹8,000; (iii) goods costing ₹4,000 destroyed by fire (no insurance claim); (iv) goods costing ₹2,500 given as charity; (v) the proprietor took goods costing ₹3,000 for personal use.", a: "(i) Furniture A/c Dr. 36,000\n        To Modern Furnishers 36,000\n(ii) Anil Dr. 8,000\n        To Computer A/c 8,000\n(iii) Loss by Fire A/c Dr. 4,000\n        To Purchases A/c 4,000\n(iv) Charity A/c Dr. 2,500\n        To Purchases A/c 2,500\n(v) Drawings A/c Dr. 3,000\n        To Purchases A/c 3,000\nThese are not credit purchases or sales of goods and not cash transactions, so none of them fits a special book; they go to the journal proper.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Kaur Stationers recorded these transactions in June: cash sales ₹18,000; credit sales to Lakhan ₹9,000 and to Mona ₹6,500; Lakhan returned goods worth ₹1,000; a computer was bought on credit from Digi Hub for ₹32,000; goods of ₹14,000 were bought on credit from Naresh.\n(i) What is the total of the sales book?\n(ii) In which book is the return by Lakhan recorded, and on the basis of which document?\n(iii) In which book are the computer purchase and the purchase from Naresh recorded?", a: "(i) Only credit sales of goods go to the sales book: 9,000 + 6,500 = **₹15,500**. The cash sales ₹18,000 go to the cash book.\n(ii) **Sales return book**, ₹1,000, on the basis of a **credit note** sent to Lakhan.\n(iii) The computer is an asset, not goods, so it is recorded in the **journal proper**. The purchase from Naresh is a credit purchase of goods, so it is recorded in the **purchases book**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "bank-reconciliation-statement": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A bank reconciliation statement is:", options: ["An account in the ledger", "A statement prepared on a given date", "A book of original entry", "A part of the trial balance"], answer: 1, explain: "It is only a **statement** that explains the difference between the cash book and pass book balances on a given date; no ledger account is involved.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The pass book is a copy of the customer's account in the books of:", options: ["The auditor", "The customer", "The tax department", "The bank"], answer: 3, explain: "The **bank** keeps the customer's account and the pass book (bank statement) is its copy.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When starting with a debit balance as per the cash book, cheques issued but not yet presented for payment are:", options: ["Deducted", "Ignored", "Added", "Treated as bank charges"], answer: 2, explain: "The bank has not paid them yet, so the pass book balance is higher than the cash book balance; they are **added**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A favourable bank balance in the cash book is shown by a:", options: ["Credit balance", "Debit balance", "Closing stock figure", "Opening cash figure"], answer: 1, explain: "A **debit** balance in the bank column means the firm has money with the bank. A credit balance is an overdraft.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these items is first recorded in the pass book?", options: ["Bank charges debited by the bank", "Cheques issued but not presented", "Cheques deposited but not collected", "Wrong totalling in the cash book"], answer: 0, explain: "**Bank charges** are known to the bank first, and the firm records them later. The others are first recorded by the firm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Balance as per the cash book is ₹30,000 (Dr.). Cheques of ₹7,000 were issued but not yet presented and cheques of ₹4,000 deposited were not yet collected. The balance as per the pass book is:", options: ["₹41,000", "₹33,000", "₹27,000", "₹19,000"], answer: 1, explain: "30,000 + 7,000 (not yet paid by bank) − 4,000 (not yet credited by bank) = **₹33,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The pass book shows a credit balance of ₹56,000. Items not yet in the cash book: a customer's direct deposit of ₹6,000 and bank charges of ₹400. Cheques deposited but not collected are ₹5,000 and cheques issued but not presented are ₹9,000. The balance as per the cash book is:", options: ["₹47,200", "₹66,600", "₹46,400", "₹45,600"], answer: 2, explain: "Start with 56,000. Less direct deposit 6,000 (pass book already shows it). Add bank charges 400 (cash book has not deducted them). Add uncollected cheques 5,000. Less cheques not presented 9,000. Result = 56,000 − 6,000 + 400 + 5,000 − 9,000 = **₹46,400**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A bank reconciliation statement is prepared by the business and not by the bank.\nReason (R): A bank reconciliation statement is only a statement and not an account.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both are true, but being a statement rather than an account does not explain **who** prepares it. The firm prepares it to check its own cash book against the bank's records.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: The accountant of Rawat Traders finds that the cash book shows a bank balance of ₹18,000 (Dr.) and the pass book shows ₹20,500 (Cr.). A cheque of ₹3,000 issued to a supplier has not been presented. He explains the remaining difference by an item that appeared first in the pass book and reduced the pass book balance. That item is:", options: ["Interest of ₹500 credited by the bank", "A cheque of ₹500 issued but not presented", "A direct deposit of ₹500 by a customer", "Bank charges of ₹500 not yet recorded in the cash book"], answer: 3, explain: "18,000 + 3,000 − 500 = 20,500. A ₹500 item that reduces the pass book balance and is known first to the bank is **bank charges**. The other options would increase the pass book balance.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: The cash book of Sethi & Sons shows a bank overdraft of ₹12,000 (credit balance). A cheque of ₹5,000 issued to a supplier has not yet been presented.\nThe overdraft as per the pass book is:", options: ["₹7,000", "₹17,000", "₹12,000", "₹5,000"], answer: 0, explain: "The cash book has already recorded the payment, so its overdraft is larger than the bank's. Overdraft as per pass book = 12,000 − 5,000 = **₹7,000**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): If the cash book shows an overdraft, cheques deposited but not yet collected are deducted to arrive at the pass book overdraft.\nReason (R): Such cheques have already reduced the overdraft in the cash book but not in the pass book.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. As the pass book overdraft is **larger** by these cheques, they must be **added**, not deducted, so A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "The pass book shows an overdraft of ₹14,000 (Dr.). Cheques of ₹6,500 were issued but not presented; cheques of ₹9,000 deposited were not collected; interest on overdraft of ₹450 was debited by the bank and not yet entered in the cash book; a customer's direct deposit of ₹2,000 was credited by the bank and not yet entered in the cash book. The overdraft as per the cash book is:", options: ["₹11,050", "₹13,050", "₹14,950", "₹16,950"], answer: 1, explain: "14,000 + 6,500 (cash book overdraft larger) − 9,000 (cash book overdraft smaller) − 450 (cash book has not recorded the interest) + 2,000 (cash book has not recorded the deposit) = **₹13,050**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The cash book shows a debit balance of ₹35,200. The pass book shows: bank charges ₹350, direct deposit by a customer ₹4,800, dividend collected by the bank ₹1,500, insurance premium paid under standing instruction ₹2,200, and a customer's cheque of ₹3,000 dishonoured, none of which are in the cash book. The corrected cash book balance is:", options: ["₹35,450", "₹36,450", "₹33,950", "₹35,950"], answer: 3, explain: "35,200 − 350 + 4,800 + 1,500 − 2,200 − 3,000 = **₹35,950**. Receipts are added; charges, premium and the dishonoured cheque are deducted.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The cash book shows a debit bank balance of ₹40,000. Later it is found that the credit (payment) side of the bank column was under-cast by ₹1,000, and cheques of ₹6,000 issued have not yet been presented. The balance as per the pass book is:", options: ["₹47,000", "₹35,000", "₹45,000", "₹33,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Under-casting of the payment side overstates the balance by ₹1,000, so the correct cash book balance is ₹39,000. Adding the cheques not presented gives 39,000 + 6,000 = **₹45,000**.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Is a bank reconciliation statement a part of the ledger? Give a reason.", a: "No. It is only a **statement** prepared outside the double entry system to explain the difference between two balances; it is not an account.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What does a debit balance in the pass book indicate?", a: "An **overdraft**: the customer has withdrawn more than he deposited and owes the bank. It appears as a credit balance in the cash book.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A cheque of ₹5,000 deposited by the firm is dishonoured by the bank. How does this affect the cash book and the pass book, and how is it treated in the BRS?", a: "The bank debits the customer's account, so the pass book balance falls, but the firm learns of it later. The cash book has still counted the cheque as a deposit, so its balance is higher by ₹5,000 until corrected.\nIf the cash book is not yet amended, ₹5,000 is **deducted** while starting with the cash book (debit) balance.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The balance as per the cash book is ₹24,000 (Dr.). Cheques of ₹4,000 issued have not been presented for payment. Find the balance as per the pass book.", a: "Cheques issued but not presented are added: 24,000 + 4,000 = **₹28,000** (credit balance as per pass book).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the cash book and the pass book (three points).", a: "(1) The **cash book** is prepared by the business; the **pass book** is prepared by the bank.\n(2) A favourable bank balance is a **debit** balance in the cash book but a **credit** balance in the pass book.\n(3) The cash book is a book of original entry in the firm's books; the pass book is a copy of the customer's account in the bank's books.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "From the following, prepare a bank reconciliation statement as on 31 March: balance as per cash book ₹45,000 (Dr.); cheques deposited but not yet collected ₹7,500; cheques issued but not yet presented ₹11,000; bank charges ₹250 shown in the pass book only.", a: "Bank Reconciliation Statement as on 31 March\nBalance as per cash book (Dr.)                     45,000\nAdd: Cheques issued but not presented              11,000\n                                                   56,000\nLess: Cheques deposited but not collected   7,500\n      Bank charges                            250   7,750\nBalance as per pass book (Cr.)                     48,250", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The pass book shows a credit balance of ₹32,000. Cheques of ₹5,000 deposited have not been collected; cheques of ₹8,000 issued have not been presented; dividend of ₹1,200 collected by the bank is not yet entered in the cash book. Find the balance as per the cash book.", a: "Start with pass book balance (Cr.)                  32,000\nAdd: Cheques deposited but not collected             5,000\n                                                    37,000\nLess: Cheques issued but not presented   8,000\n      Dividend collected by the bank     1,200       9,200\nBalance as per cash book (Dr.)                      27,800", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prepare a bank reconciliation statement as on 31 March starting with the cash book balance of ₹62,400 (Dr.): (i) cheques issued ₹14,000 not presented; (ii) cheques deposited ₹9,800 not collected; (iii) bank charges ₹450; (iv) interest allowed by the bank ₹1,350; (v) insurance premium of ₹3,200 paid by the bank under standing instruction; (vi) a customer's direct deposit of ₹6,000. Items (iii) to (vi) are not yet entered in the cash book.", a: "Bank Reconciliation Statement as on 31 March\nBalance as per cash book (Dr.)                      62,400\nAdd: Cheques issued but not presented     14,000\n     Interest allowed by the bank          1,350\n     Direct deposit by customer            6,000    21,350\n                                                    83,750\nLess: Cheques deposited but not collected  9,800\n      Bank charges                           450\n      Insurance premium paid by the bank   3,200    13,450\nBalance as per pass book (Cr.)                      70,300\nCheck: 62,400 + 21,350 − 13,450 = 70,300.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "On 31 March the cash book shows a bank overdraft of ₹20,000. Items not yet in the cash book: interest on overdraft ₹800 and bank charges ₹300 debited by the bank, and a customer's direct deposit of ₹5,000. Also, cheques issued ₹12,000 have not been presented and cheques deposited ₹7,000 have not been collected. (a) Find the corrected cash book overdraft. (b) Prepare the BRS to find the overdraft as per the pass book.", a: "(a) Amended cash book balance:\nOverdraft as per cash book (Cr.)                  20,000\nAdd: Interest 800 and bank charges 300 (credits)    1,100\nLess: Direct deposit (debit)                        5,000\nCorrected overdraft (Cr.)                          16,100\n(b) BRS:\nOverdraft as per amended cash book                 16,100\nLess: Cheques issued but not presented            12,000\n                                                    4,100\nAdd: Cheques deposited but not collected            7,000\nOverdraft as per pass book (Dr.)                   11,100\nReason: the cash book has already recorded the issued cheques (making its overdraft bigger) and the deposits (making it smaller).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: On 31 March 2025 the cash book of Kapoor Cycles showed a bank balance of ₹56,400 (Dr.). The accountant noticed that cheques of ₹8,000 issued had not been presented for payment, cheques of ₹6,200 deposited had not been credited by the bank, and the bank had charged ₹450 which was not entered in the cash book.\n(i) Which of these three items were first recorded in the pass book?\n(ii) Find the balance as per the pass book.\n(iii) What is the correct bank balance to be shown in the balance sheet?", a: "(i) Only the **bank charges of ₹450** were first recorded by the bank (in the pass book). The other two were first recorded in the cash book.\n(ii) Pass book balance = 56,400 + 8,000 − 6,200 − 450 = **₹57,750** (Cr.).\n(iii) The correct balance is the cash book balance after entering the bank charges: 56,400 − 450 = **₹55,950**.\nCheck from the pass book: 57,750 − 8,000 + 6,200 = 55,950.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
